   ### Implementing the API for having the all available posts. 
   Whatever the post on database, whenever the user go the homepage, then we would like to listdown the all the available post. So we have one of the challaenges that we need to focused is that, If there is  one million posts, we donot want to have the situation that all the post would be fetched at one shot, payload is havey and the frontend which loading the payloadhas to process a lot of post. And there is no need to post the one million post because no user will be abale to see the one million post in one shot. Probabily able to see the 20 to 30 posts. 

   Currently having the one api by which we are able to ceatePost i.e app.post('/posts', s3uploader.single('image'), createPost). And we need to have one more api which can be able to fetch all the api. 
   - now we are having only two api, later may be we are haing the 200 api, are we are going to list all the api in this main server file, where we are having the all server going on.
   - So we are missing the one layer in our project is routing layer, so now we are having the more than one api so it is good time to ceate the routing Layer. 

   ## Routing Layer 
   - To create the routing layer in the express so there is something called the express router(google search). 
   - Routing: is the mechanism with which our application is going responds to the client request. For this routing we have the express.Router. 
   - UseCase of the express.Router :- is used to create the moduler , mountable , routehandler. The word moduler says everything we want to ensure that we have the routing mechansim that is cleaned enough to segregate our logic properly. We have the sepertae module that handle the routing together. 
   
   Router in express :- 
   How router looks like? http://localhost:3000 -> base address. http://localhost:3000/posts -> We cannot understand by seeing this is the as it is API url where we make  some request and get some response. Or it is websites url. 
   - So segregate the API routes and non api routes so will be prepends our routes with /api i.e http://localhost:3000/api/posts  
    - By doing so anyone can understand that it  is the api url. 

    Note :- If we are having the api that has been written in the graphQl then in these case we cannot put /api beacuse in grapghql we are having the single graphql url. 

    API Versioning :- 
    - API : using the api you can expose the business logic to the outer world so that people can cosume your logic. At the point covid, gov of India release some API(rest api), you can able to fetch all the nearby hospital where vaccine is available. 
    - similar API, like for the upi, user payment Interface that we using in India, The NPCI the organisation that actually controls all about the upi. Actually exposes the lot of API. This is not public but 3rd party app can lile g-pay can use this API. 
    - So if the Api expose are not so Optimized it may be having the security risks,etc then now they want to release the another version of the API. 
     - In the impovement of the api, there might be the chance that the req and res structure would be change. Then due the change in the req and res structure , the client have to also change in their logic. 
    - So client, not got bothered, during the updation of api for  that we have api versioning. In the api versioning we release the multiple version of the api, like 3rd parties app using the version 1 of the api, then npci upgrades those of api, then npci relases the version 2 of api,  NPCI gives the some time to all the 3rd parties apps, NPCI decleared that they have changes the req and  res structure of the api In given time period please migrate from version 1 to version2. During this period they will be also supporting the versin 1 of the api. 
    - visit the dunzo website. Inspect->network->headers -> request url.  

    How we can use the express Router ? 
    1. Create router object : So require express object and using this object call the Router method this will return the router object. 
    2. Similarly we are doing the app.post... calls , here we do with router.post..  . 
    3. And then export the router 
    4. Embed the router as a middleware. 
    5. Call it in the index.js file as app.use() -> here we pass the two parameter
       1. '/posts': strating of the url
       2. Redirect kaha karna hai
       - This line index.js means if the url starts with /posts then use the postRouter to handle the request
       - In the postRouter we will look at the remaining url part after /posts ka baad jo bhi hoga usko dekhenge, so ham just '/' denge as hamko jis tarah ka request hoga us ko ham handle kar lenge. 

    - We have seen now that our index file farwarding the request to the router. but we can also farward the request from the index file to router and from these router to another router. Hence Nesting of route can be done here. 

    - We can do app.use('api/v1/posts', postRouter) in index file , and farword it to the router but this way of implementing is good for the small scale project but not for the large scale. For example if different version of the api is come then we have to write again in the index file so ye phir pahle jaisa ho jayega jiske liye hamne router banaya tha mesh kam rahe .
      - So ab ham karenge ki agar koi bhi request aata hai /api par then we call the apiRouter -> app.use('/api', apiRouter)
      - phir api router se userRouter aur postRouter ko call kar denge on the basis of reuest. 

      Note :- All the inner router will bw using the router.use() method. So this will be checking if the remaining part of the url is start with this, jo bhi string hai uske ander. 
      - And the very last of the router will be having the http method or type of request we were doing. 

  ### Writing the api to get all the posts.  
  So this api will be fetching the posts but it is not fetching the post directly all the posts from our database, idealy it should fetch the posts in the paging network fashion. So like if having the 100 posts then we it should fetch 10 posts on first network call and next 10 posts on next call and so on. So on every network call we will be fetching the next 10 posts.

  In order to make the paging network api how the api looks like? 
  api/v1/posts?querryparams -> generlly we use the query params
  properties of querryParams :- 
  limit=10 :- No of records we want to send
  offset=0 :- starting the recods drom where we want to send the records
  api/v1/posts?limit=10&offset=0

  getALLPost() :-
  1. Fetch the limit and offset from the query params. 
  2. Now we need to pass this limit and offset to service layer, things can be fetched. 
  
  lets see how the database querry for the paging concepts looklike? There are also limit and skip method in mongoose, default method exist. 
  - we use the .limit(10) to limit the amounnt of fetching data
  - we use the .skip() , so agar first 10 records chahiye toh skip ko 0 kardo which means ki do not to skip any post and skip 10 means pahle 10 ko chod do uske baad wale ko posts fetch karke lao

  - We need to show the latest data, so we would like to sort the data in the descending order. So we have the method in mongoose for sort() go to documentation to see how can we use this method. 

  - To show the total no of pages, the extra info which could be shown by the user on their ui page -> countDocument() in the mongoose. 

  - const totalDocuments = await posts.countDocuments() : so we donot want our model  to interact directly interact with service layer. so we made the method countAllPosts in repositories. 

  - If have the total documents then we can figure out the number of pages. 
  - We can also calculate the value of current page on the basis of offset. 

### Writing the api for update post
- so we have the function, findByIdAndUpdate() in the mongoose , go and serach about it on the documenttaion. 

1. Create the method in the postRepository for updating the post
2. Then go to the service layer and call repository function there
3. Then go the controller layer and call the service layer function there
4. Then go to the router layer and register the path for this function. 

- In the postRepository we just call the ODM/ORM function of the mongoose
- In the postService layer we do the some manipulation with the data we got in the function by the controller function. 
- In the controller layer, we fetch the required data from the params 

```js
//  This method will be update the document but it return the old post
export const updatePostById = async (id, updateObject) => {
    try {
        const post = await Post.findByIdAndUpdate(id, updateObject)
        return post
    } catch (error) {
      console.log(error);
         
    }
}
``` 
- using this {new : true} only we can ensure, it stops the mongoose to return the old document it always return the new updated document, when the update has been done : 
```js
const post = await Post.findByIdAndUpdate(id, updateObject, {new : true} )
```

### Validation layer :- 
So we are left with the validation layer, as if now if someone creating the post we donot make necessary that what is atlest need to create the post like caption is must or an image should be there. 

- We can also write the customs validation that we can build. or We can use third party libraries is classValidators, zod, etc. We can do the manual based we see later. 
- We are going to implement with zod, so it is the typescript based library. 
- After installing the zod, we try to the setUp the middleware. 

zodValidaor.js :- 
- The function validate have the callback function which expect :- 
  - schema : zod says that for any request you can define the schema kind of an object, so it is the expected input properties for the particular request body. 
  - using this function we return the another function which is the kind of middleware which have the try catch. 

  - schema.parse() : schema is the zod created object. This object will have the parse property. So inside the parse fun we can pass the javascript object and it will check whather the incoming javascript object is validating the schema or not. If req passed then it go for the next layer if not then it through an error. 
   - In zod we have the custom logic our file schema validation we can serach on google and take the reference from there. 

   - Issues while Implementing :- 
     - So the multer has the capability to read the form-data from the client postman, So we are validating the request before it reaches to the multer. 
     - so we are able to validate the urlencoded data

     Solution for this problem  :- 
     1. Using the multer instance
      - Is approch ko use karne ke baad ham form-data ke through caption toh change kar paa rahe hai, but image bhejte samay error aa raha hai. 
      - unexpected error has been come :-  so we need to properly configure this multer , as what the necessary requirement we need to check as we do on the multerConfif.js. 
    
    2. Validate ham directly, multerConfig mai he karte hai. 

    - So we have the problem for zod and multer combining is that 
       - We donot want zod validation to go before the aws upload
       - and zod is not able to read the image because multer has not been configured
       So it is kind of deadLock 

       Resolve :- So we put the zodPostSchema only validating the caption. 

       - And for the file validate/image we go to the multerConfig and validate their directly wheather the image has been come proper format or extension. 
       - In the postController, we have set the resposne to send on the network that Image is Required.
       So we have done the manual validation for the image. 
       - We can also add the MIME Check, if the file is not particular type then you show them to send the response status accordingly. 
  
  How exactly the validation is happening? 
  - So firstly we have the common middleware, Inside the common middleware we write the function validate and this function expect something which called schema. And schema is actually an object that we send through zod so it the also called the zodSchema. In the zodSchema we have the all the relevent property mentioned thet we expect to come from the request body. Zod schema object is going to pasrse the request body and see/check if it is validating or not. If it is validating then we move to next middleware that is going to be our controller, if not validating then it through an exception and the catch function will catch the exception and we send the response of the error. 
  - Creating the zodSchema object : 
    - import the z from the zod
    - the z object has lot of propertirs we use the z.object()
    - z.object : take the another object as an parameter
      - inside we mention the what kind of validation we need. 
  - In the routing layer : When we doing the post, we are validating the caption of incoming request. 
  - We are manually validating the image

  ## Authentication :-  
  Before Coming to the authentication part we would like to do the PreRequistic for the authentiaction that id Registration. 

  ### Registration :- 
  - It is the process by which user register , him/her self on the plateform on the first time. or Registration is the process with which a user provides their basic details for the very first time to the platform. 
  - To fulfill the registration we have the signUp api:- 
     - So the signUp api is going to create the new data on the database. So signup api is generally a post data.
     - api looks like :- POST:/api/v1/signup
  - It will be expecting the request body.
    - Inside the request body we have email, password , userName, etc property will be there. 
  
  So when Someone hit this api, technically to things happen : 
   1. We stored the data of the user on the db
   2. Any kind of sensitive information like password,etc we transform it in the non-original form.
     - We do so for the mishappening with owner
       - AnyOne who have the access of the database can misuse 
       - if database can got leak and in any form
       "Hash the sesitive information"

  - We have created the createUser() in the userRepository 
  - In the signupUserService() method : 
     - Here will be getting the user related details in the  user object
     - There are two ways :- 
       1. Seperately make the database call, and check whather the there is already a user having the  corresponding email or not. 
       - Technically we do not need to do that because when we try to create, user from the code base we have writte using the schema then and there is already a username and email So that will not allow to create the user. (unique : true)
       - So we do not need to call the seperately database call by doing the findUserBYEmail because that will create the unnecssary load in the database, we doing db call, we can do in one db call.
    - We need to hash the password in some compatible format. 
        - we take the plain passwrod from contol layer to service layer
        - In service layer we modify plain password in the hashpassword
        - and then store the password in the database 
        - There is two way to implement it :- 
          1. Write the all of its logic in service layer, and should not go the repository layer because it is meant to be the plain db calls 
          2. So we want to change/modify one of the property of the incoming request object just before it stord in the database.
          - Most of the database provide the mechansim called triggered. Triggeres are the event handlers, whenever something is about to happens in your database, then we can track this eventa and do something specific. 
          - So database provide us something which called the triggers, and triggers are kind of event handlers, which are excuted on the different different event. Event may be just before creating the data, just after creating the data, just deleting the data, just after deleting the data, just before updating the data, etc. 
          - Triggers help us to track and attach event handlers to it. Do something specifice just before the is going to created. 
          - So this concept will be in the everyDatabase, might be chances that name of this concept ka naam kuchh aur ho. In mongoose they are called hooks -pre and post.
            -> These hooks are basically attached with the schema. 
        - Trigger, just before user creation in database and modify our password in this triggers.  
  
    - userSchema.pre :- 
      - before saving the data, we call the callback function which is general function.
      - the callback function has the access to next middleware chain for mongoose
      - In the function we get the access to userObject
        - Accessing incoming user access, the function  has the access of the this keyword, the this keyword points to the userobject from which we are going to call the schema, means incoming userObject, if try to access the this keyword hereyou will be getting the access of incming user objects which is the plain password. 
        Note :-  If we not make the normal javascript function, if use arroe function then that arrow function will create the problem to us because in arraoe function the this keyword not resolve in this way. 

      - Now we are able to access the user plain pasword now we have to encrypt the password for that we  have the library bcrypt. So technically it is a algorithum, to implment that we have the libarary called bcrypt. 
      - When you have the data and you want to change the data from one form another, we use a lot algorithum. One of the algorithum is bcrypt. These algorithum takes the different ways to change the data from one form to another. 
      - saltRounds : A parameter of bcrypt, take the numeric value. It is the cost of processing the data. More the number of saltRounds, more will be the processing done by bcrypt to hash tha data. So stronger will be the hash created. 
      - Hashing: Most of the hashing algorithum are generally one way. Means that any password that is comming up and we convert them in the hash password. If someone give the same password then then we create the same hashPassword. But hashpassword se wapas password nahi pa sakte. 
      - So we have the concept called collision, where multiple key may have the same hash value, if want to reverse engineering then you donot know that your hash value convert to what key. 

      - Checking if the user send same request of signup again then in this what should be the error should show to clientside.
       - From the userRepository through the error as ham wahi se user create hone ke liye database mai request kar rahe so If data already exist there then database sabse pahle response toh yahi bhejega. 
       - so hama yaha se throw karenge and catch karenge userService mai. and wo phir se error through kar dega, ab error controll layer mai bhej denge aur res ka status 500 bhej dega.  So we have the different error handling nulit in function/properties of the mongoose we can search on the google-> documentation. like name, messages, etc

    - whenever we add the unqiue : true constraints to the schema then what internally mongoose does is to identify , wheather this property is uniqe across docement of the collection, mongoose create internally called an index. Index in database is a data structure that databases prepare to do a lot  of things like faster search, find unniue search more quickly, .   


### Authentication :- 
- It is a process where we verify that user is same one, who they clam to be. 
- Lets say if user want to delete the post, so we allow only whome , who own that post. 
  - So if i created the post then i should allow to delete the post and I cannot delete any other's post. But to implement all the post, firstly system needs verify who am I. 
- Authentication can be Implemented in different different mechanism :-
  - Most used michanism is, Token based authentication :- 
    - If you signed to the plateform then the plateform gives you the token, now you as the  user is responsible for storing the token. Now everytime, once you recive the token, every time when we have to interact with server/plateform for anything, we are going to send the token with your request. 
    - The server side then validate the token. then we are able ti access the resources only. 
    - This token is act as a key card of he hotel, using this token only people will be identify that yes this is valid uer of the plateform. 
    - This token may expire, we may to relogin after 5 or 7 days. Or if loose the token then we have to again signup the plateform. This kind of authentication is called the token based mechanism. 

   - Inorder to implement the token based authentication, the most general way to implment is JWT : JSON Web Token. 
   - JWT is a type of token that we can issue to the user. There is also different type of token also exist rather than JWT. 
   - This is widely used mechanism becacuse JWT is kind of token, that is capable of storing data inside it. And we are able to destructure/fetch this data from the token on the server side. 
   
   - JWT token :- 
      - It is dot sepereted three component token, which is in hexadecimal/alpha-numeric form. 
      - Header : Provide the some extra piece of information about the tpken. like what algo we are actually using to convert the data in the JWT token. 
      - payload : It is data, that we are going to encode or store inside the JWT. Data can be username, id and email of the user. We donot need to store the password as there is not the use of that, as when use the token,and we use token again and again. 
      - signature : It is very crucial part, because to create the signature we use the secret-key, The secret-key only store at server side. So until they know our secret key, they are not able to decode JSON  web token. And we need the same secret key to convert the jwt to the same payload. 
       - The secret-key take our header and made the base64url string with that. Take dot and takes the payload beas64URL . And then then takes the secret-key. Combined all of this to create new alpha-numeric hash.  (jwt.io pa jakar dekh sakte hai ki jwt kaise kaam karta hai.)
        
      - And we also have the jsonwebtoken libary in node :
      - jwt.sign() method : It takes the payload, privatekey/secretkey and we have to gaive algorithum if we donot give the algo, then it takes the default algo. 
      - If we have the token and we want to decode on the server side then we have :
        - jwt.verify method : pass the secretkey, it will decode it. If unable to decode then token then it throw an error. 

  - How we are implement this api ?
    - /users/signin:post 
    - for sign we use email and password -> Comes from the req.body because if use query params or url params then it will be stored in the history.
    
    1. Check if the user with same email exists or not?
    2. Check if the passwrord given matches the hassed password or not?
       - bcrypt will help here, having the bcrypt.compaire() -> takes the plainPasseeord of the client , hashPashword from database and a function.
       - so here the we hash pasword ko convert nahi kar rahe hai, plainPassword mai we just pass and it will gives the result. 
       - bcrypt takes the planePasswrod and convert it to hash then that hash is compaire with hash of database.
    3. Issue a jwt token to a user. 

    Now , If user wants to create the post on plateform?
    1. User sends JWT token on the plateform
    2. We check if the JWT is not malformed
    3. Authenticate the user by fetching the payload from the JWT, which will tell us the identity of the user
    4. Then on behalf of the user we will do the operation

  -> Implementing authentication    
  - In the userService :- 
    - We create the function, signinUserService. Inside function :-
    - We check wheather user exist or not. We doing on the basis of email which is uniqely identify the user.
    - If user exist, then compare the password, we compare password using bcrypt. 
    - If password is not vaild then we say, unathorized (401) password.  
    - If user and password exist then we will generate new JWT tokens. 
      - Install the jsonwebtoken 
      - create new folder utils
      - create funnction generateJwtToken, which takes the payload as a parameter
        - import jwt from jsonwebtoken
        - jwt.sign() -> Takes : 1. payload 2. secret-key 3. callback function
    - call the generateJwtToken
    - return token 

  - In userController, create signin(). Inside the dunction : 
    - Use try catch
      - here we call the signinUserService and send the req.body as parameter
      - send the status code and successfull message. 
    - In catch, if status code from service layer then, we send the custom message. 
  
  - register the router in user.js

  - In zodSigninSchema.js create zodSignInSchema. 
    
 